{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791387382,"nonce":"FUei72LssYT8PB9vAhZGuFSPDvs=","message":"Implemented dense RPO-indexed CHK intersection and lazy dominance frontiers. Frontier predecessor snapshots preserve query results after source mutation and cloning. The independent path-removal oracle covers all 512 four-block CFGs without entry/self edges, including disconnected and irreducible cycles. Empty-entry and frontier-lifetime regressions added.\n\nValidation: 419 compiler unit tests; 6 bounds-version, 17 expression-lowering, 3 loop-specialization, 14 OSR-transfer integration tests; 43 Lua and 50 Python conformance categories with LLVM off and on. The three complete/official Lua suites were excluded from the focused runs; an accidental initial broad run was stopped, not counted as passing.\n\nNUC direct old/new analysis costs, five alternating rounds in one process, generated chains with joins and backedges (medians, microseconds): 16 blocks 6.006 -\u003e 4.686 (-22%); 128 blocks 52.725 -\u003e 36.079 (-32%); 1024 blocks 439.828 -\u003e 284.033 (-35%). Raw evidence /tmp/zyntax-dominators-cost-nuc.log; experiment /tmp/zyntax-dominators-cost.rs. These are analysis timings, not whole-program speedups.\n\nSame-executable old/new control: method_call wall 127.209 -\u003e 122.077 ms; repeated 125.838 -\u003e 123.291 ms. Nbody and chaos mixed/no clear gain. A between-build FFT first-iteration regression also occurs with old analysis enabled in the same new binary, so it cannot be attributed to the dominator algorithm. Lowering/optimizer traces and GDB sampling investigated it further. Default-threshold FFT first iteration ~426 ms versus saved baseline ~302; threshold=1 diagnostic gives ~308 ms for both. Four normalized native streams for FFT transform, entry, resume and adapter matched between original/new analysis builds. Full-size FFT (1024, 1000 cycles) printed matching six rounded values and sum 1014.184705 for both Zyntax builds and CPython, across two iterations. Additional output changes the timing discrepancy, so do not claim a measured general Python improvement.\n\nInvestigation identified a separate Cranelift egraph loop-context gap tracked in 785c10b. Isolated dependency patch improves invariant placement; performance acceptance is still in progress. No default thresholds or frontend lowering changed here. NUC chaos checksum run that hit disk quota is invalid; after removing four superseded experimental binaries from this thread, its checksum rerun passed.","files":null}]}